#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# central_moment

#% text
# `central_moment(d, k)` gives the `k`-th central moment `E[(X - mu)^k]` of a distribution; `central_moment(xs, k)` gives `sum((xs - mean)^k)/n` of a data list.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
central_moment(normal_distribution(mu, s), 4)
#% output
#= 3*s^4

#% cell
central_moment([1, 2, 3, 4], 2)
#% output
#= 5/4

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# The second central moment of a distribution is its variance:

#% cell
central_moment(poisson_distribution(m), 2)
#% output
#= m

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `moment`, `variance`, `skewness`
